---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import BaseConverter from '../../components/BaseConverter.astro';
import BaseTable from '../../components/BaseTable.astro';
import { guides } from '../../data/guides';

const guide = guides.find((g) => g.slug === 'number-system-conversion-calculator-with-solution')!;

const toc = [
  { id: 'what-solution-means', label: 'What a solution includes' },
  { id: 'method-one', label: 'Method 1: positional expansion' },
  { id: 'method-two', label: 'Method 2: repeated division' },
  { id: 'fractions', label: 'Fractions' },
  { id: 'tool', label: 'Calculator with solution' },
  { id: 'naming-solution', label: 'Solutions for lakh and crore' },
  { id: 'world', label: 'Reference grid' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is a number system conversion calculator with solution?',
    answer:
      'A calculator with solution returns the result <em>and</em> the method: the positional weights that produce the decimal value, and the division chain that produces the target base. That makes the answer checkable and teachable, which is why it is the format exams and code reviews expect.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do you convert 255 to hexadecimal with a solution?',
    answer:
      'Divide repeatedly by 16: 255 ÷ 16 = 15 remainder 15 (F); 15 ÷ 16 = 0 remainder 15 (F). Read bottom-to-top: <strong>FF</strong>. In binary the same value is 11111111, because each hex digit maps to four bits.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does 0.1 decimal not convert exactly to binary?',
    answer:
      '0.1 has no finite binary expansion — it becomes 0.0001100110011… repeating. Any calculator that prints a fixed number of fraction digits is rounding, not failing. The step list should show the repetition so the rounding is visible.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can a solution show the lakh/crore conversion too?',
    answer:
      'Yes, but the steps are multiplicative rather than divisional. For example, 2.5 billones (Spanish, 10¹²) = 2.5 × 10¹² = 2,500,000,000,000; divide by 10¹² for lakh crore → <strong>2.5 lakh crore</strong>.',
  },
];
---

<Fragment slot="head">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'HowTo',
    name: 'Convert a decimal number to another base with the full solution',
    description: 'Divide the integer part by the target base, record every remainder, then read the remainders bottom-to-top.',
    totalTime: 'PT3M',
    tool: [{ '@type': 'HowToTool', name: 'NumSys base converter calculator' }],
    step: [
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Divide', text: 'Divide the integer part by the target base.' },
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Record the remainder', text: 'Write down the remainder as the next least-significant digit.' },
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Repeat', text: 'Continue with the quotient until it reaches zero.' },
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Read upward', text: 'Read the remainders bottom-to-top to get the answer.' },
      { '@type': 'HowToStep', name: 'Check', text: 'Convert the result back to decimal using positional weights and confirm the original value.' },
    ],
  })} />
</Fragment>

<GuideLayout guide={guide} toc={toc} faqs={faqs} toolHref="#base-converter" toolLabel="Open the calculator">
  <h2 id="what-solution-means">What a number system conversion calculator with solution should print</h2>
  <p>
    “Show your working” is a classroom rule that turns out to be good engineering practice. A <strong>number system conversion calculator with solution</strong> should produce three things: the result, the method that produced it, and enough intermediate values that a second person can audit it without redoing the whole calculation.
  </p>
  <div class="answer-box">
    <p><strong>The minimum viable solution:</strong> the source base, the decimal value, every division or multiplication with its carry or remainder, and the final digit string with the direction of reading stated.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    The two methods below are the standard ones. Learn both and you can convert in either direction without a tool — and you can spot a wrong answer from a tool in a few seconds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method-one">Method 1: positional expansion (any base → decimal)</h2>
  <p>
    Each digit is multiplied by the base raised to its position, counting from zero at the right of the integer part. Negative positions apply to the fraction.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked solution: 1A.8₁₆ → decimal</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Digit <code>1</code> at position 1: <code>1 × 16¹ = 16</code></li>
    <li>Digit <code>A</code> (10) at position 0: <code>10 × 16⁰ = 10</code></li>
    <li>Digit <code>8</code> at position −1: <code>8 × 16⁻¹ = 0.5</code></li>
    <li>Sum: <code>16 + 10 + 0.5 = 26.5</code></li>
  </ol>
  <p>Answer: <strong>1A.8₁₆ = 26.5₁₀</strong>. The same expansion works for octal (<code>777₈</code>), binary (<code>10011100₂</code>) and base 36.</p>

  <h2 id="method-two">Method 2: repeated division (decimal → any base)</h2>
  <p>
    Divide the integer part by the target base, record the remainder, and continue with the quotient until it reaches zero. Read the remainders bottom-to-top. For radix breadth beyond the four common bases, see the <a href="/knowledge-catalog/base-converter-calculator/">base converter calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked solution: 173₁₀ → hexadecimal</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><code>173 ÷ 16 = 10</code>, remainder <strong>13 → D</strong></li>
    <li><code>10 ÷ 16 = 0</code>, remainder <strong>10 → A</strong></li>
  </ol>
  <p>Read bottom-to-top: <strong>AD</strong>. Cross-check with binary: <code>1010 1101</code>, and with octal: <code>255₈</code>.</p>

  <h3>Worked solution: 200₁₀ → binary</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><code>200 ÷ 2 = 100</code>, remainder 0</li>
    <li><code>100 ÷ 2 = 50</code>, remainder 0</li>
    <li><code>50 ÷ 2 = 25</code>, remainder 0</li>
    <li><code>25 ÷ 2 = 12</code>, remainder 1</li>
    <li><code>12 ÷ 2 = 6</code>, remainder 0</li>
    <li><code>6 ÷ 2 = 3</code>, remainder 0</li>
    <li><code>3 ÷ 2 = 1</code>, remainder 1</li>
    <li><code>1 ÷ 2 = 0</code>, remainder 1</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Read bottom-to-top: <strong>11001000₂</strong> = 128 + 64 + 8 = 200. The forward and reverse directions agree.</p>

  <h2 id="fractions">Fractions: the part most calculators hide</h2>
  <p>
    For the fractional part, multiply by the target base and take the integer carry, then repeat with what remains. Read the carries top-to-bottom.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><code>0.6875 × 2 = 1.375</code> → carry <strong>1</strong>, remainder 0.375</li>
    <li><code>0.375 × 2 = 0.75</code> → carry <strong>0</strong>, remainder 0.75</li>
    <li><code>0.75 × 2 = 1.5</code> → carry <strong>1</strong>, remainder 0.5</li>
    <li><code>0.5 × 2 = 1.0</code> → carry <strong>1</strong>, remainder 0</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Answer: <strong>0.6875₁₀ = 0.1011₂</strong>. When the remainder never reaches zero — as with 0.1 — the solution should say so and show the repeating block rather than pretending the expansion terminated.</p>

  <h2 id="tool">Use the calculator with solution</h2>
  <p>
    The tool below prints the expansion and the division chain for any base pair from 2 to 36, including fractions. The result panel shows the answer; the step panels show the audit trail.
  </p>
  <BaseConverter />

  <h2 id="naming-solution">Solutions for lakh, crore, million and billion</h2>
  <p>
    Naming conversions do not use division by a base, but they do have a reproducible solution format: identify the scale exponent, multiply to a plain value, then divide by the target scale exponent.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked solution: 2.5 billones (Spanish) → lakh crore</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Spanish <code>billón</code> = 10¹², so <code>2.5 × 10¹² = 2,500,000,000,000</code>.</li>
    <li>1 lakh crore = 10⁵ × 10⁷ = 10¹².</li>
    <li><code>2,500,000,000,000 ÷ 10¹² = 2.5</code>.</li>
    <li>Answer: <strong>2.5 lakh crore</strong>, which is 2.5 trillion.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked solution: 1.5 mil milhões (Portuguese) → crore</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><code>mil milhões</code> = 1,000 × 10⁶ = 10⁹.</li>
    <li><code>1.5 × 10⁹ = 1,500,000,000</code>.</li>
    <li>1 crore = 10⁷; <code>1,500,000,000 ÷ 10,000,000 = 150</code>.</li>
    <li>Answer: <strong>150 crore</strong> (1.5 billion).</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked solution: 5 crore → million</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>1 crore = 10⁷; <code>5 × 10⁷ = 50,000,000</code>.</li>
    <li>1 million = 10⁶; <code>50,000,000 ÷ 1,000,000 = 50</code>.</li>
    <li>Answer: <strong>50 million</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="world">The reference grid behind every solution</h2>
  <p>
    Base conversion does not depend on language. Radix and naming are separate axes: if a solution crosses a language boundary, check the 10⁹/10¹² split in the
    <a href="/knowledge-catalog/number-system-conversion-table/#world-table">world numbering table</a>, then come back to the base arithmetic. The 0–15 grid is the working set for most classroom solutions.
  </p>
  <BaseTable to={15} caption="Decimal, binary, octal and hexadecimal for 0–15 — the working set behind most solutions." />

  <h3>Fractions: state the rounding rule</h3>
  <p>
    0.1 in decimal is 0.000110011… in binary. Any solution that prints a finite string is rounding; the honest format is to show the repeating block and state how many digits were kept. The same applies to 0.2, 0.3 and any fraction whose denominator has a prime factor other than the target base’s.
  </p>

</GuideLayout>
